e on the defensive\n- After Kd2 or Kf1, I can continue developing with tempo\n\n**Reason:** \nWhite's King on e1 is vulnerable, and their Queen on f3 is somewhat out of play for defensive purposes. By checking with Qh4+, I force White's King to move, permanently losing castling rights. This gives me the initiative and disrupts White's coordination. The Queen will be actively placed on h4, controlling key central and kingside squares while my opponent deals with King safety issues.\n\n**Answer: Q: d8-h4**"}
